# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone** — no equivalent needed
3. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** — catch block is dead code

## Modern .NET 8 Implementation

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token); // pass token to unblock
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait with timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Log warning: worker didn't finish within timeout
            // No forced kill — design GetNextItemAsync to respect the token
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a `BlockingCollection` or socket, cancellation won't interrupt it. Pass the token to the blocking API (`Take(token)`, `ReadAsync(token)`, etc.). |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Unobserved exceptions vanish. Store the `Task` and `await` it in `StopAsync`. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | It can't unblock a waiting call. `CancellationToken` integrates with async waits and `WaitHandle`. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` for coordination** | Replace with `await Task.WhenAny` + timeout — non-blocking and deterministic. |
| **No forced termination exists** | .NET 8 has no equivalent to `Abort()`. Design all blocking operations to accept and honor a `CancellationToken`. This is the fundamental architectural shift. |

## If You Must Support Sync-Only Blocking APIs

```csharp
// Wrap a non-cancellable blocking call with a dedicated thread
private Task<WorkItem> GetNextItemAsync(CancellationToken token)
{
    return Task.Run(() =>
    {
        // Poll with short timeouts so cancellation is checked
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            if (_queue.TryTake(out var item, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(500)))
                return item;
        }
        throw new OperationCanceledException(token);
    }, token);
}
```

## Summary

The core design change: move from **"pull the rug out" (`Abort`)** to **"ask politely and wait" (`CancellationToken`)**. Every blocking call in the loop must participate in cooperative cancellation — there is no backdoor to force-terminate work in modern .NET.
